{
  "beruf": "kraftfahrer",
  "title": "Driving and delivery",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "italian-for-work run, 2026-11-15: written by hand for Italy, not yet read by a human or a second reader. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation. Working conditions are general orientation only; facts marked 'to verify' in the run report.",
  "intro": "Delivering parcels, food and goods is a common job in Italian cities and along the motorways, by van, scooter, e-bike or lorry. This deck has the vehicle, your daily work, routes and deliveries, the rules and papers, and what you say at the door and to customers. For the driving test itself, see the patente vocabulary.",
  "introEasy": "Do you deliver parcels or food, or drive for work in Italy? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Corriere",
    "Autista di furgone",
    "Autista di camion",
    "Fattorino/a",
    "Rider"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "At the door, one short sentence is enough: «Buongiorno, ho un pacco per la signora Rossi. Può firmare qui?»",
    "Through the intercom (citofono), say who you are and what you bring: «Buongiorno, sono il corriere. Ho un pacco per l'interno 5.»",
    "Driving for work can need more than the patente B: a heavier lorry needs the patente C and usually the CQC, and lorry drivers have rules on driving and rest times (to verify, they depend on the vehicle). A foreign licence is not always valid for long in Italy. Ask your employer or the Motorizzazione; this is general orientation, not legal advice.",
    "Delivery riders may work as employees or as self-employed people (lavoratore autonomo, often with a partita IVA). Pay, insurance (INAIL) and rights differ: ask the platform, a union (sindacato/patronato) or the Ispettorato del lavoro before you sign; this is general orientation, not legal advice."
  ],
